WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.545 --> 00:00:01.670
IRFAN BAQUI: Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.000
Welcome to Apigee Up Close.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.080
I'm Irfan Baqui.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.510
I'm a Customer Engineer
with Google Cloud.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.240
I work very closely
with our customers

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.600
to help them make
better Cloud decisions,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.530
Cloud architectural decisions,
and also better API management

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:18.280
decisions.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.140
And today, we'll be
talking about how

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.500
to think beyond the gateway.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.080
So let's start with
the objective, right?

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.690
What we'll be discussing
today, and what

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.060
you'll be getting
out of this talk

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.030
is how to be successful
with your APIs,

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.470
and have a well-thought-out
strategy driving your API

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.230
program.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:45.030
And just to clear a few
buzzwords here, by strategy,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.910
I really mean looking
at not just your APIs,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:51.330
but your entire business,
and looking at the metrics

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.360
that you're using to drive
your business forward,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.030
and connect your
APIs to help you

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.440
achieve those metrics, right?

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.060
So that as a whole is
what we call a strategy.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:07.560
And an API program is
just a collaboration

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.180
between your multiple
groups, multiple functions

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.750
within your organization to
really drive the strategy.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.160
So here is the agenda.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.770
I'll start by
setting the context

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.000
by giving an example of what
a successful API program looks

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:24.510
like.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.300
And I'll do it through
telling you about one

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.340
of our customers that
has been thinking very

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.190
innovatively about
the API strategy,

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:39.060
and their journey through
multiple years on iterating

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.150
through it and really being
successful in the API program.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.870
Then, we will pivot
and we'll talk about

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.110
how APIs fit into your
company's architecture,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.760
and what different
approaches you can

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.480
take to build your API program.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.210
Security, as we see
it, is a core component

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.250
of any API strategy, we'll
talk about that as well.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.280
And finally, we'll
tie all of this

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.330
together into the main
subject of today's call,

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.699
how to look beyond
the API gateway,

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:12.570
and really think about
API management as a whole

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.310
to drive the API program.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.860
And then we'll end it with
questions and answers.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.420
Let's dive right into it, right?

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.930
So the successful API program
I want to talk to you about

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.610
was of Walgreens.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.350
And it's a very
interesting story.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:34.360
Back in the days, Walgreens
started as a classic brick

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.870
and mortar chain of pharmacies.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.140
And really, it was no different
than any other mom and pop

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:41.950
pharmacy.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.360
The difference was the scale.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.110
They had more than 8,000
stores, and the metric

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.750
that they boasted
was that they had

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:57.690
a store within five-mile
distance of 75% of the US

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:58.830
population.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.390
That would have been a
success criteria, right?

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.080
Because that's how you
could tell how many people

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.130
Walgreens could reach.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.160
And if they were able
to reach those people,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.990
they would be able to
sell things to them,

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.600
and that's how
they're successful.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.200
But with the advent
of the digital age,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.050
we're all familiar
with how Amazon

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.230
has been disrupting a lot of
different retailers, right?

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:29.880
The classic story is how Amazon
started disrupting Walmart,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.010
and how Walmart really had
to form a unique strategy

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.005
to fight back.

00:03:36.005 --> 00:03:37.380
Walgreens was a
little different.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.400
They were ahead of the
game, and it basically

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.580
started as somewhat
of what I would

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.172
call a coincidence, right?

00:03:47.172 --> 00:03:48.880
So within the Walgreens,
if you have ever

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.430
been in one of the stores,
they have a photo lab

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.950
where people, back in
the days, would go in

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:58.910
to develop the photo rolls and
get the pictures out, right?

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:04.310
So with the advent of
smartphones around 2008, around

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:10.640
that time, people stopped
using the photo-roll cameras,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.820
and the smartphones
were smart enough

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.970
to take pretty decent
quality images at that time.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:22.490
So one of the ways
Walgreens was benefiting

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.950
from the photo labs was
that people would come in

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.995
to develop their
pictures, and as a result,

00:04:27.995 --> 00:04:29.870
they would buy something
else as well, right?

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.340
So the foot traffic was
very beneficial to them.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.970
But with the advent
of smartphones,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.840
then that had gone down.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.890
So they needed to think
about the customer

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.490
and what they wanted,
why did they go away,

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.100
and figure out how
they can get them back.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.570
Turned out that
they were looking,

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.010
they were still
looking for a way

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.140
to print their digital photos.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.400
So what they did was they
created a native application

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.490
to let the end users upload
their pictures to the app,

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:04.480
and pick them up developed
at a nearby Walgreens store.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.530
To do this, they had to create
and expose the necessary APIs

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.230
to the internal
developers, so that they

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.120
could create that application.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.530
So that was good.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.450
That restored some
of their traffic--

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:17.890
not all of it, right?

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.390
So they said, all
right, is there

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:23.110
another, bigger opportunity
for us to get more foot

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:25.400
traffic into our stores?

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.450
So they looked at how exactly
were the end users interacting

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.280
with their photos, right?

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.200
What additional things
were they doing it?

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.090
What other additional
applications

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.370
were they using to
enhance their pictures?

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:42.040
It turned out that
there were a plethora

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:45.220
of other mobile and
web applications

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.490
where users would
upload their photos.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:52.090
For example, for
holiday cards, and they

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:54.850
would decorate the card with
their photos and all of that.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.950
Ultimately, they would
want to print it out.

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:01.240
So they said, all right, how can
I tap into those applications,

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.120
and somehow incentivize
the developers

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.780
of those applications to
have those photos printed

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:12.490
at a nearby Walgreens, right?

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:15.880
It solved their problem of
getting the photos printed,

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.120
and it gave more foot
traffic to Walgreens.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.950
So it was, in their mind,
a win-win situation.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:26.170
So what they did was now,
they expanded the API program

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:31.570
to expose the APIs to partners
so that they could incorporate

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:35.150
them into their application,
and really created an ecosystem.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:39.670
So this ecosystem, it
started with photo APIs

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:43.360
and expanded into a
bunch of other services

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.850
that they could offer,
like prescription refills,

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.340
to medical and health
care applications,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.330
like purchasing their in-store
merchandise with a partner

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:56.740
like Postmates, like
integrating Walgreens reward

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:01.100
programs into other partner
applications, and all of that.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.946
So now with expanding
into an ecosystem,

00:07:05.946 --> 00:07:10.090
they were able to really
extend their reach

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:14.080
from one or a handful
of native applications

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.490
into hundreds of
partner applications.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:17.185
And that's huge.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:25.840
So that was, as a whole, an
example of a successful API

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.070
program, right?

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.850
But what made Walgreen's
API program successful?

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.490
I think the simple
answer is that they

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:37.280
had a relentless focus on
developers and customers,

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:37.780
right?

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.940
They started with
their customers first,

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.280
they thought about what
exactly they wanted,

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.790
and they gave it to them.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:47.800
And the method of giving it
to them was through APIs,

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.830
but it was still connected
to their business strategy.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.460
There were two essential
parts that played a huge role.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:59.330
And one of them was a developer
portal and incentives.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.260
And this is how
they communicated

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:05.470
with their developers,
with their partners.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.230
In addition to just
exposing the APIs,

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:12.250
they also exposed an
incentive program to them.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.940
They said that hey, for
every dollar, for example--

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.570
I'm just making numbers up--
but for every dollar of revenue

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:23.870
that you bring in to the
photo prints API, for example,

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:27.030
I will incentivize you $0.10.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:30.420
So now, there were
a lot of developers

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.809
that were making [? net ?] new
photo applications just to make

00:08:33.809 --> 00:08:36.480
revenue out of this program.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:37.919
So it was very popular.

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:40.320
The second one was analytics.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.720
Once the APIs were
being consumed,

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.190
the way they really gauged how
the users and the developers

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.950
were accessing,
consuming the APIs

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:55.680
gave them intel on how they
could make them better.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.250
So these two components
were crucial,

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:01.160
beyond just creating
and exposing those APIs.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.970
So let's tie this back into how
you should think about your API

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.940
program, right?

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.590
There are multiple
different approaches

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:12.290
to building an API
program, but it really

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:14.340
boils down to two major ones.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.010
One is a tactical approach.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.310
And the other one is a
more strategic approach.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.350
So let's talk
about both of them,

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:28.330
and see what are the
pros and cons, right?

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.660
So let's talk about
the tactical one first.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.880
Let's make it a fun
exercise, all right?

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:39.640
Let's say you have a CEO
or CTO of your company come

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:41.740
to you to say that,
hey, I've got an idea.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:46.750
We have this API service
that we should really

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.810
broadcast out to our partners.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:53.460
It would be a mutually
beneficial move, both to us

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.170
and the partners, right?

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:59.110
And he gives you the
task to make that happen.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:09:59.920
How do you do it?

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.880
So let's say now
you are following

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.255
the tactical approach.

00:10:04.255 --> 00:10:06.630
The first thing you'll do is
you'll identify the service,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.810
and you'll say, hey, how do I
expose this out to, let's say,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.720
just one external
application for now.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:18.010
And the solution you come to is
just open up a firewall port,

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:18.510
right?

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.260
And obviously, you
will need some security

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.260
in front of the
service to make sure

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.120
that you're allowing
just the people that

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.970
should be allowed to access it.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.860
But that's a solution.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.260
All right, you open
up the firewall port,

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.340
you add the security
policy there.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:36.240
The next thing is, what about
rogue applications, right?

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.750
They can really
crash your system

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.620
if they call too frequently.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:44.980
In that case, you would just add
a rate limiting functionality

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.540
and call it the day.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:50.480
Next, what if each
API call needs data

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:51.890
from multiple backends?

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:54.450
How would you take care of that?

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:59.130
Let's say you have some
orchestration being performed,

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:03.080
so that an API call comes
in, talks to three, four,

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.270
any number of
different backends,

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:07.610
gets their response
back, matches it up,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:12.300
and passes it back to
that calling application.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:19.055
Now, do you see what might be
a problem with this strategy?

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.900
What's not in focus here?

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.780
Well, the customer is
not in focus, right?

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:32.980
You are really thinking about
that particular service,

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:34.780
and how you would expose
it out, but you're

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:38.560
not really thinking about
how to-- who is the customer,

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.040
and what experience you
actually want to give them.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.180
Now, let's take a look
at a more strategic way

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:49.700
to look at your APIs.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:51.770
What you're seeing
on the screen right

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:57.590
now is what we call a digital
value chain, which encompasses

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:02.420
all of the different entities
that would eventually,

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:06.355
in one way or the other,
interact with your APIs.

00:12:06.355 --> 00:12:07.730
On the left, you
have your users.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:10.220
On the right, you have your
backend systems, right?

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:14.540
So traditionally,
what people did

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:20.310
was think about it from more
of an exposure point of view.

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:23.207
And that's what I illustrated
in the previous example.

00:12:23.207 --> 00:12:25.040
You have a service, and
you're just thinking

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:27.380
about how do I expose this out?

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:29.120
I would mitigate
some of the problems

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.100
around it, like security
and rate limiting

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.410
that we talked about,
but essentially, you just

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:34.770
want to expose it out.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.160
But on the other hand,
the success pattern

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:40.370
that we have seen is
the opposite, right?

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:43.670
Think about the users first.

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:47.930
So when your user is
sitting on the table,

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.510
opening up their
laptops or interacting

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:53.990
with the mobile application,
what is the experience that you

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.470
want to give them?

00:12:56.470 --> 00:13:00.130
Once you have identified
that particular experience,

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:05.900
then you have to think about
what kinds of applications

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:09.140
would give them those
experiences, right?

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:13.320
And those applications would
then be made by developers.

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.260
So essentially, you
want to make sure

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:19.160
you're giving absolutely
awesome APIs to developers,

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.500
so that they don't need to
worry about the complexities

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:25.188
of your systems, and they can
really focus on the experiences

00:13:25.188 --> 00:13:26.855
that they're building
for the end users.

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:33.120
And that's really in the
hands of an API team.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.600
And a lot of you
on the call might

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.540
be part of such an API team.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.540
You would really
make sure that you're

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.020
focusing on the end user,
and also on your developer

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:49.050
first, and creating
those APIs, designing

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:55.610
those APIs such that they
are addressing their needs.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:58.880
And eventually then, you
would connect those APIs

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:01.130
to your backends, right?

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:05.000
That's where all of the
interesting data and logic

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.652
of your enterprise resides.

00:14:06.652 --> 00:14:09.110
So you would connect them to
the back ends to get the data,

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:12.480
execute the logic,
and all of that.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:16.370
This is how you would
strategically view your APIs,

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:19.820
but this also poses
a problem, right?

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.550
And that problem is from
a security standpoint.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:26.420
We talked about all
of these entities

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:30.560
being part of the
digital value chain.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.230
But the more entities
you have, the more effort

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:35.990
you need to put in to make
sure that they're secure.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:42.770
For example, on the left, you
can have a legitimate end user,

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.040
or you can have a hacker,
or you can have a bot.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:50.990
Some bad actor that really
wants to abuse your API.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:55.070
You can have really any
application on the internet

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:59.480
interacting with your
APIs, so how do you

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:03.200
identify that it's the
right application that you

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.150
have authenticated?

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.840
How do you do provision
access of that application

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:10.190
to your system if needed?

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:12.200
So all of that's important.

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:16.010
On the developer side, who
are the developers that

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.720
are consuming your APIs, right?

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.100
Are they registered?

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:20.180
Do you know them?

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.740
Do you-- are you able
to track them well?

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:27.290
And if needed, are you able
to drop them from API program?

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.030
Are you able to
deprovision them?

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:33.330
For APIs, are they secure
enough at runtime, right?

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:36.080
Are they following the best
practices and standards

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:40.640
that your organization has laid
out, both from a regulatory

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:42.750
and from a compliance
standpoint?

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.740
And finally, your
backend systems.

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:49.970
Is the data coming in and
out of your backend systems

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:52.340
coming in and out securely?

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:53.870
Is it secure?

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:55.610
Is it encrypted addressed?

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:57.110
Is it encrypted in-flight?

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.230
And all of that.

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:00.890
And how do you also
make sure that you're

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:05.420
protecting your backend
systems from a certain overload

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.610
in the API call volume?

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.610
So let's look at how an
intelligent API management

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:15.320
platform would mitigate
some of these threats,

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:19.910
and make sure that
your strategy is also

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:22.850
taking care of security.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:26.150
So at Apigee, we actually
make sure that each

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:29.630
of these entities are
thought about cohesively.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.730
For example, for
the end user, you

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:33.710
would need
behavior-based security

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.600
to identify the
patterns how they're

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:39.890
calling you or your
APIs, and classify them

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:42.990
as either bad actors or
legitimate users, right?

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.260
You would also want
signature-based security

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:50.780
to protect your systems against
the well known OAuth attacks.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:53.330
And you might also want to
do some fine-grained control,

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:58.190
like looking at the payload
complexity, and making sure

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.800
that adheres to a
particular schema

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:04.470
that you have defined
for requests coming in.

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:07.670
You may also want to
protect your backend systems

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:09.650
from some abusive
users, if they're

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:13.520
calling the APIs too frequently,
and have them spike arrested.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:16.609
So you can do all of that
on the end user side.

00:17:16.609 --> 00:17:18.109
Also for the
applications, you would

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:21.109
want to make sure that
you're identifying them,

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:24.109
you're authenticating them
through industry standard

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:28.760
or OAuth2 protocols, and you are
restricting them or authorizing

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:33.970
them to access just the APIs
that you give them access to.

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:36.790
Now for developers, you
may want to make sure

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:40.600
that these are your trusted
employees or partners.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:43.210
And to do so, you
can authenticate them

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:47.440
through an identity provider,
like Okta or PingFederate.

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:49.570
Now talking about
your APIs and API,

00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:56.080
teams you'd want to make sure
that your API teams have access

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:00.190
to only the APIs, and even the
functionalities within those

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:03.220
APIs that they should
have access to.

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.350
And teams, like
your security team,

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:08.530
are able to set blanket
policies for all of your APIs,

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:10.360
to make sure that
your system is secure.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:14.530
You'll be able to do all of that
with an API management platform

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:15.820
like Apigee.

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.280
Lastly, talking about
your backend systems,

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:22.060
you'd want to make sure
that the connection to them

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:26.710
is secure through something like
mutual TLS and IP whitelisting,

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:30.040
and the data that
flows in and out

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:33.460
is encrypted, both on
the wire and a trust.

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:38.110
So we talked about how to
really look at your API program

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:40.660
strategically, right?

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:43.510
Thinking about the
users first, and also

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:46.460
thinking about security
as you are doing it.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.530
So what exactly is needed
for you to be able to do so?

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:54.460
The first is through
developer management, right?

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:57.760
Having a portal
where your developers

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:03.070
can come in and learn more
about your API program.

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:06.370
Maybe part of it is
your documentation.

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:11.500
Other part of it is monetization
incentives to use your APIs.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:14.620
Maybe it also has an integration
to your support portal.

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:18.010
Whatever that is,
but any way for you

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:23.370
to quickly onboard and engage
your partners and developers.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:24.900
Next is analytics, right?

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.690
So as they start
consuming your APIs,

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:31.950
you want to see how exactly
they're consuming it, right?

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:34.710
Where are the end users located?

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.240
How are they using
the applications?

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.720
So you can look at
all of that data

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.220
and make your API
program better.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:46.050
The next is
operations automation.

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:49.520
So as your API program
grows and scales,

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:54.050
you shouldn't really need to
worry about the operations

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:55.850
yourself, right?

00:19:55.850 --> 00:19:59.300
You worry about the APIs, you
worry about your products,

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:01.400
and the operations
and everything else

00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:04.590
should just happen.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:06.990
Next is API development.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.870
As you're developing these
APIs, one thing to note

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:14.910
is that they would have to
be developed quickly, right?

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:18.960
Your developers and
their innovation

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:23.820
actually depends on how fast
you are rolling out features.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.550
So that would mean
that ideally, you

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:30.750
would have prebaked policies,
like we provide in Apigee.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:33.840
Drag and drop, really
quickly change the APIs,

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:38.490
iterate them, version them,
and go to market faster.

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:42.560
And finally, a secure
and flexible API

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:47.210
runtime, so that you can
process the incoming requests

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:50.380
and the responses going out.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:54.030
So all of this
together is really

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:59.377
a core part of a good
API management platform.

00:20:59.377 --> 00:21:00.960
These are features
and functionalities

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:04.110
actually that you get
right now in Apigee.

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:09.120
And one thing to note is
that a very tiny portion

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:12.880
of this functionality
is an API gateway.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:17.490
So tying this back to the
original idea of our talk

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:20.430
today, thinking beyond
the API gateway,

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:24.450
when you really think about
the users first and make sure

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:30.060
you are tying your API strategy
into your business strategy,

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:35.040
then you would need an
intelligent API management

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:39.500
platform to drive
that API program.

